# Protections for the env/PATH-hijack corpus. Held IDENTICAL across every target
# in this family on purpose: the *reachable sink the overflow lands on* is the
# only variable, so a protection difference would confound the measurement.
#
# These mirror the measured target (HTB `sabotage`,
# tests/htb-targets/a12c7382-edc2-4554-ab36-fe26b48f8fd4/challenge/sabotage),
# whose checksec reads PIE / canary ON / NX / RELRO FULL. That combination is
# deliberately the FULL modern set, because this category is data-only: nothing
# in the exploit path corrupts a return address or a code pointer, so every one
# of these protections is satisfied while the target stays trivially exploitable.
# Building the family hardened keeps "hardened" from being quietly re-learned as
# "not exploitable".
#
# -fstack-protector-all  matches the measured target: sabotage's vulnerable
#                        function enter_command_control() loads fs:0x28 and
#                        checks it before returning. The overflow never touches
#                        the frame, so the canary is irrelevant -- which is
#                        exactly the point of keeping it on.
# -pie -fPIE             as in the target. No exploit step needs a code or heap
#                        address, so PIE costs the attacker nothing here: every
#                        offset used is a fixed intra-heap distance.
# -Wl,-z,relro,-z,now    Full RELRO, as in the target. There is no GOT write in
#                        this category.
# -O0                    the primitive depends on readBuffer() re-reading its
#                        base pointer and index from the frame each iteration
#                        and on Malloc()'s `n + 8` actually being computed at
#                        run time; at higher -O gcc constant-folds the wrap
#                        check away in the negative control and the family stops
#                        being a controlled comparison.
-fstack-protector-all
-pie
-fPIE
-Wl,-z,relro,-z,now
-O0
